5.7 Comparison with a Time-Series Foundation Model
We benchmarked TimesFM [16], a decoder-only foundation model pre-trained on a large time-series corpus, in two regimes (Table 7). Parameter-efficient fine-tuning with a LoRA adapter [17] (r=6, α=12) on its hidden state gives the best window-level scores in the study (0.977/0.969AUROC). Usedzero-shot, however—a frozen feature extractor with a logistic-regression probe—it reaches only87.6%/84.4%accuracy and0.940/0.910AUROC, below even the compact interleaved encoder trained from scratch. General time-series pre-training therefore does not by itself capture PD-specific structure; clinically specific adaptation is required to discriminate look-alike movement disorders.
5.8 On-Device Latency
With minimal preprocessing overhead the deployed model sustains124.5ms per window—only6.2%of the2s realtime budget—leaving ample headroom for continuous on-device monitoring. This efficiency follows directly from the fusion choice. The interleaved encoder issues a single self-attention operation per block over the concatenated2T sequence—three multi-head attention calls in total, against the twelve of explicit cross-attention—and uses159,556 parameters versus cross-attention’s418,180(2.6×fewer), at no cost in accuracy (Table 3). Measured over the same eleven streamed recordings, explicit cross-attention costs260.1ms per window, roughly2.1×slower, while late concatenation costs119.6ms and a single-wrist encoder75.2ms, quantifying the price of bilateral sensing. On-device power consumption was not measured and is left to future work.
5.9 Limitations
Our study uses a single dataset (PADS); validation on an independent, multi-site cohort is necessary before deployment, and the patient-disjoint partition must be preserved throughout to avoid leakage. Self-supervised pre-training is performed on the same data used for fine-tuning, which likely understates the benefit of contrastive learning; a fairer test would pre-train on a large external corpus of unlabelled IMU signals. PADS lacks uniform severity and time
